WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:05.460
Howdy folks. My last video got a lot&nbsp;
more attention than I was anticipating,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.420
and I have a couple of non-MTG&nbsp;
videos I’m doing research for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:14.280
so I figured I may as well put out a quick little&nbsp;
video with a few more thoughts on the EDH format.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:20.460
Today I want to talk about a few different ways&nbsp;
of orienting a deck in relation to its commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:23.940
and one in particular that&nbsp;
I consider to be underrated.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:31.260
To start with, let’s talk about a pretty&nbsp;
conventional way of constructing a commander deck.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:37.140
You find a commander that looks cool, you find&nbsp;
cards that synergize well with said commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:40.740
and then you fill in the gaps with the&nbsp;
other thing the deck needs to succeed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.500
This is a simple and malleable format,&nbsp;
but there are other options out there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:52.800
To talk about those, a useful exercise is&nbsp;
to think about the rules of the commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:58.260
format as if you’ve never seen them before.&nbsp;
You’re building a 99 card singleton deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:04.620
something with a naturally high degree of&nbsp;
variability, but you have an ace up your sleeve:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:12.000
a card that you will always have, every single&nbsp;
game. You could make that card Krenko, Mob Boss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:18.360
and then play a goblins deck. And that’s a&nbsp;
perfectly valid plan. But something you might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:24.960
notice about that idea is that the commander and&nbsp;
the deck do basically the same thing—play goblins.

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:31.020
A lot of strong decks will opt to have the&nbsp;
commander do something tangential to the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:36.720
the deck, tying in with some key cards but broadly&nbsp;
offering something different in nature than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:42.720
rest of the deck. However, my favorite form of&nbsp;
this relationship is where the commander does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:49.320
the exact opposite of the thing that the rest of&nbsp;
the deck is trying to do. Basically you pick out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:54.720
a commander, and then you build a midrange-y deck&nbsp;
with a glaring weakness solved by that commander.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:01.080
An example that comes to mind is a Glissa&nbsp;
Sunslayer deck that I built several months back.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:07.980
The card is a fantastic creature for&nbsp;
three mana—an absurdly effective blocker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:12.480
a draw engine, and a source of endless&nbsp;
sadness for decks running enchantments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:18.600
The EDHRec page seems to consist mostly of decks&nbsp;
trying to capitalize on removing counters from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:25.380
your cards, but I instead opted to build a&nbsp;
hardcore battlecruiser deck. The commander’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:30.960
flexibility and daunting presence means that I can&nbsp;
fully forget about early game creatures and not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:37.440
worry as much about draw, instead jamming my deck&nbsp;
to the brim with ramp, removal, and massive cards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:43.920
I have a top-notch blocker that’s also a draw&nbsp;
engine every single game, and that lets me use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:48.720
my deck slots to focus on being the greedy&nbsp;
bitch that I truly want to be deep inside.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:54.660
Another example of this is a&nbsp;
Liesa deck that I built recently.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:01.140
When I first saw Liesa, I went “god, that card&nbsp;
could absolutely beat the tar out of people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:05.760
All I want in this world is to mercilessly&nbsp;
beat my opponents with that angel.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:14.220
So great, my commander is selected. How to build a&nbsp;
deck around it? Well, judging by the EDHRec page,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:19.560
most people’s answer to that question&nbsp;
is to put it in a lifegain deck. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:25.260
I don’t hate this approach. Y’know, run a bunch&nbsp;
of cards that gain life and some lifegain synergy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:31.080
and a commander that will sap life out of both you&nbsp;
and your opponents. It makes sense, and you could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:35.700
even argue it sort of fits into my template&nbsp;
here. But that’s not the direction I went.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:44.760
Instead, I decided to build a deck that’s just&nbsp;
dying, craving for a beater to cut through a stale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:52.920
boardstate—I built a hatebears deck. I crammed as&nbsp;
many hate bears as I possibly could into the deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:59.280
just truly an upsetting wall of little asshole&nbsp;
guys trying to ruin the day of my poor opponents.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:04.980
And the deck has ended up working exactly&nbsp;
as intended in a good portion of games—the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:12.060
cards in the deck chip in damage, prevent damage&nbsp;
coming at me, and generally hinder my opponents in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:19.020
all sorts of ways, and then Liesa cuts through the&nbsp;
resulting board quagmire to reduce my opponents to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:25.920
a paste-like homogenate. By design, it’s&nbsp;
a deck with roughly a Liesa-shaped hole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:31.440
and I’m able to play Liesa for 5 mana&nbsp;
anytime I want in any game I want.

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:39.420
There’s a lot of room for variation here, but this&nbsp;
gives a decent idea of the basic formula. Running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:44.220
a commander that covers a particular element of&nbsp;
the deck allows a player to build a deck that puts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:50.280
that element on the backburner, instead focusing&nbsp;
all its effort on the things that it knows it can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:55.320
succeed at. This way, you can effectively&nbsp;
just solve the problem you know your deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.320 --> 00:05:00.120
will have ahead of time instead of agonizing&nbsp;
over how many deck slots to devote to it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:05.820
I certainly don’t like the prospect of figuring&nbsp;
out how to kill my opponents consistently with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:10.140
a low to mid-budget hatebears deck,&nbsp;
so I just avoided that question.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:16.200
Midrange is often something that people&nbsp;
look down on for being boring or bland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:21.180
but with some creative usage of the rules of&nbsp;
the format you can build a really fun deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:26.224
that exists within the midrange realm, and&nbsp;
I think more people should try doing that.

